<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jang Bahadur Rana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1817–1877</span> · Also known as Jung Bahadur Rana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jang Bahadur Rana was a Nepalese general and statesman who seized power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-massacre">Kot massacre</a> of 1846 and founded the Rana regime, under which his family held hereditary office as prime ministers while the kings were figureheads. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1850, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a> law code in 1854 and sent troops to help the British during the Indian Rebellion of 1857. The Rana regime lasted until 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana.jpg" alt="Jang Bahadur Rana" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr John William Tyler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jung_Bahadur_Rana_in_1887.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
